messages:
ada: Heads-up: a customer on Cloudflare Workers hit a runtime error using our JS SDK in an ESM project — error says "ReferenceError: fetch is not defined" even though workers provide fetch. Repro repo linked.

marco: that's been showing up in a few sandboxes. The problem is twofold: runtime resolution of fetch and TypeScript lib types (RequestInit/Response missing for consumers who don't enable lib.dom).

rachel: can we prefer globalThis.fetch when available and fallback to undici for Node? That pattern usually covers Node 16/18 and edge runtimes. Also Deno has a different import story so we should be careful with bundling.

tom: sketch of runtime-safe approach I used elsewhere:
```ts
// runtime/fetch-resolve.ts
export async function resolveFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch !== 'undefined') return globalThis.fetch;
  // dynamic import so bundlers for workers that don't have undici won't include it
  try {
    const undici = await import('undici');
    return undici.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available in runtime; see https://redwood.example/sdk/fetch for polyfills');
  }
}
```

nada: That looks good. For sync-init code paths (like top-level SDK constructors) dynamic import could be stripped by some bundlers (Next.js edge). We should add a synchronous guarded error message that points to docs rather than crash with obscure stack.

marco: about types: publish a tiny ambient declaration file we include in the package to avoid forcing consumers to set lib.dom. Example: `types/fetch.d.ts` with `declare function fetch(input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit): Promise<Response>;` — keep it minimal so it doesn't collide with consumer DOM types.

rachel: and ensure our build emits that declaration in dist and package.json's types points to it. That prevents the missing symbol TS error for folks who keep "lib": ["es2020"] in tsconfig.

tom: export map wise, double-check package.json exports so require/import consumers get correct bundles. Something like:
```json
"exports":{
  ".":{
    "import":"./dist/index.esm.js",
    "require":"./dist/index.cjs.js"
  }
}
```

nada: CI additions: run a small matrix job that does 1) node cjs smoke, 2) node esm smoke, 3) deno run of example, 4) cloudflare worker minitest via wrangler dev or worker runtime test. Failures should be triaged before publishing.

ci-bot: automated note: opened issue RDW-7699: sdk: improve fetch/runtime/type compatibility and add CI smoke tests.

marco: one more subtlety — bundlers that polyfill node fetch sometimes provide a different signature. We should avoid depending on internal fields of Response and use standard-safe patterns. Also document recommended polyfill for Node: `globalThis.fetch = (await import('undici')).fetch` in app entry.

rachel: let's also add a small section in quickstart showing two patterns: edge (no polyfill) and node (polyfill snippet). That reduces onboarding friction. I'll draft the copy.

tom: I'll prepare a tiny patch with resolveFetch + throwing diagnostic message and a unit test that simulates globalThis.fetch undefined. Then follow up with a types-only PR to add the fetch ambient declaration.

ada: thanks — once both PRs are green, we can cut a patch release and update the sample app in the onboarding repo. Also add a short troubleshooting note for Next Edge that if bundlers remove dynamic imports they need to explicitly polyfill at build-time.

ada: ping me when PRs exist and I'll add the release task.

ada: tl;dr: prefer global fetch, dynamic import undici fallback, ship small .d.ts ambient for fetch, update exports, add CI smoke runs, docs quickstart.
